bination formula \\(\\binom{8}{2}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{2} = \\frac{8!}{2!(8-2)!} = \\frac{8 \\times 7}{2 \\times 1} = 28\n\\]\n\nSince there are 8 rows, the total number of ways to place the pieces in the same row is:\n\n\\[\n8 \\times 28 = 224\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Placing the pieces in the same column\nSimilarly, there are 8 columns on the chessboard. For each column, we can choose 2 out of the 8 rows to place the pieces. The number of ways to choose 2 rows out of 8 is again \\(\\binom{8}{2}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{2} = 28\n\\]\n\nSince there are 8 columns, the total number of ways to place the pieces in the same column is:\n\n\\[\n8 \\times 28 = 224\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Adding the two cases together\nThe total number of ways to place the pieces such that they are either in the same row or the same column is the sum of the two cases:\n\n\\[\n224 + 224 = 448\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{448}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{448}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:391"}
